			<dict>
				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>Lock Signal Probe — standalone diagnostic.

Wire this shortcut into two Personal Automations for ONE test app: "App Is Opened" passing the text OPEN, and "App Is Closed" passing the text CLOSE. Then exercise the phone by hand: switch apps, lock the screen with the app in front, unlock, go to Home Screen, and so on.

Every run appends one line to a Note called Lock Signal Probe Log recording the time, which signal arrived, and whether the device was locked at that instant. Comparing the CLOSE lines that carry Device Is Locked = true against the ones that carry false tells you whether iOS fires the App Is Closed trigger on screen lock the same way it fires on an app switch.

This shortcut deliberately shows no alerts, dialogs or results so it never interrupts you while running in the background.</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionIdentifier</key>
			<string>is.workflow.actions.comment</string>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionParameters</key>
			<dict>
				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>Shortcuts generated by Shortcuts Playground. May contain mistakes. Always check the shortcut's actions first.

This shortcut was created via the following user prompt:

&gt; Build a small standalone diagnostic Shortcut named "Lock Signal Probe" for the PROSOCHE project. It accepts OPEN or CLOSE as Shortcut Input from a Personal Automation (or nothing when run by hand), reads Get Device Details for Device Is Locked, formats the current date to the second, builds one single-line log entry, and appends it to a Note named exactly "Lock Signal Probe Log" (creating that note the first time). It must never show an alert, dialog or result, because it runs silently in the background whenever the phone is locked or unlocked.</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionIdentifier</key>
			<string>is.workflow.actions.comment</string>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionParameters</key>
			<dict>
				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>--- READ SIGNALS ---

ALLOW_DATETIME_FORMAT — the probe needs precision down to the second to line up a screen lock with the automation that fired next to it, so Format Date deliberately uses a custom date AND time pattern rather than a date-only string.

- Get Device Details is set to Device Is Locked
- Date is left on Current Date so it stamps the exact moment of the run
- Format Date takes its Date from the Date action above</string>
